          Question 10:  What file has the script modified so the malware will start upon reboot?

- After identifying the malware contained in the files, we now know one of the file's purpose is to establish a persistent backdoor.

- This is seen as crucial for attackers as their malicious scripts continue to inhabit the system even after it reboots allowing long term 
access.

- We need to analyze the 3.html file and carefully investigate the script written. The second line moves the file with the malicious payload
to a path called /var/mail/mail to hide it there since it is a system folder to minimize suspicion.

- The main line we are interested in is the command modifying the /etc/rc.local system file. rc.local is a traditional Linux startup script
that runs after the system reboots. So it is a clever tactic attackers use to establish persistence.

- The attacker overwrites the rc.local scripts and add their modifications that are designed to start the malware that was moved to
var/mail/mail which is the backdoor.

- Waits for 1 second to ensure the malware gets assigned a Process ID, then fetches the ID and sends it to the rootkit interface to hide it from
administrators, users and security programs. 

- The tells the system the startup script completed normally without errors (exit 0).



